\begin{abstract}
Neurosymbolic formalizers trained with Group Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO) achieve high prove rates---93\% on FOLIO---yet 87\% of proved outputs embed the conclusion verbatim among the premises, a degenerate shortcut invisible to execution reward.
We introduce \emph{Solver Trace Ablation} (STA), a diagnostic that applies leave-one-out component necessity testing to solver inputs.
The fraction of components whose removal causes proof failure yields the \emph{Intensional Structural Reward} (ISR): ISR$=$1 indicates full necessity; ISR$=$0 complete redundancy.
ISR is uncorrelated with downstream NLI accuracy ($\rho{=}{-}0.049$), confirming it measures component necessity---not semantic faithfulness.
As a diagnostic, ISR exposes a collapse trajectory hidden from PR: over 100--1000 GRPO steps (Qwen3-4B), ISR falls $0.642{\to}0.003$ while PR rises $32\%{\to}96\%$; a supervised fine-tuning (SFT) baseline reaches 92.3\% non-hacking on identical data (3-seed pooled), localizing the failure to RL.
As a reward, $r = r_{\text{exec}}\cdot(1{+}\beta\,\text{ISR})$ at $\beta{=}0.1$ induces Goodhart effects on the structural metric: 70.6\% conclusion duplication (H7) versus 13.7\% under matched random (Fisher $p{=}1.3{\times}10^{-39}$; random H7 is seed-dependent, driven by one of three seeds); ISR amplifies H7 frequency ${\sim}5{\times}$ rather than exclusively causing it; the sole apparent ISR advantage reduces to $1.06\times$ after controlling for a premise-count confound; $\beta{=}0.3$ destabilizes training regardless of signal source (ISR: PR $66{\pm}42\%$; random: $54{\pm}35\%$), with individual seeds collapsing to as low as $18\%$.
Cross-logic transfer to Prolog yields universal Goodhart (3/3 seeds, 0\% non-hacking); cross-dataset replication on ProofFOL (${\sim}$500 examples) confirms structural degeneration generalizes ($\text{ISR}^*{<}0.12$, ${\leq}1.4\%$ genuine in converged seeds), while ISR as training signal produces dataset-dependent outcomes---Goodhart collapse where hacking satisfies the metric (FOLIO) versus beneficial instability where it does not (ProofFOL; 76.8\% vs.\ 17.4\% genuine, $p{=}0.10$).
Across 6 reward conditions, 2 logic types, 2 dataset families, 3 seeds, we catalog 8 hacking modes---including query-as-fact gaming that bypasses text-match classifiers---documenting ISR's fundamental asymmetry: reliable as a diagnostic, dataset-dependent as an optimization target.\footnote{Code and data: \url{[anonymized]}}
\end{abstract}

